{"name":"Ammonite Fossil","hex":"#a58d6d","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"ammonite","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any of an extinct group of cephalopods of the subclass Ammonoidea; a fossil shell of such an animal."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["An explosive prepared from a mixture of TNT and ammonium nitrate; a form of amatol, popular in Eastern Europe and China."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/ammonite"},{"term":"fossil","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The mineralized remains of an animal or plant.","Any preserved evidence of ancient life, including shells, imprints, burrows, coprolites, and organically-produced chemicals."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/fossil"}],"entries":[{"title":"Ammonoidea","blocks":[{"text":"Ammonoids are extinct, typically coiled-shelled cephalopods composing the subclass Ammonoidea. They are more closely related to living octopuses, squid, and cuttlefish than they are to nautiluses, which their shells resemble. The earliest ammonoids appeared during the Emsian stage of the Early Devonian, around 410-408 million years ago, with the last species vanishing during or soon after the Cretaceous–Paleogene extinction event approximately 66 million years ago. They are often called ammonites, which is most frequently used for members of the order Ammonitida, the only remaining group of ammonoids from the Jurassic up until their extinction."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/00/Pleuroceras_solare%2C_Little_Switzerland%2C_Bavaria%2C_Germany.jpg/330px-Pleuroceras_solare%2C_Little_Switzerland%2C_Bavaria%2C_Germany.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ammonoidea"},{"title":"Fossil","blocks":[{"text":"A fossil is any preserved remains, impression, or trace of any once-living thing from a past geological age. Examples include bones, shells, exoskeletons, stone imprints of animals or microbes, objects preserved in amber, hair, petrified wood and DNA remnants. The totality of fossils is known as the fossil record. Though the fossil record is incomplete, numerous studies have demonstrated that there is enough information available to give a good understanding of the pattern of diversification of life on Earth, and it can fill gaps in our understanding of the sequence of evolution on earth."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1a/Fossil_Diversity.png/330px-Fossil_Diversity.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fossil"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/da/Haeckel_Ammonitida.jpg/500px-Haeckel_Ammonitida.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/da/Haeckel_Ammonitida.jpg/1280px-Haeckel_Ammonitida.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/da/Haeckel_Ammonitida.jpg/500px-Haeckel_Ammonitida.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A variety of ammonite forms, from Ernst Haeckel's 1904 Kunstformen der Natur (Art Forms of Nature)","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AHaeckel_Ammonitida.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/90/Ammonoidea_evolutionary_history.PNG/500px-Ammonoidea_evolutionary_history.PNG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/90/Ammonoidea_evolutionary_history.PNG/500px-Ammonoidea_evolutionary_history.PNG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Scheme of the stratigraphical distribution of Ammonoidea","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AAmmonoidea_evolutionary_history.PNG